A protocol is a set of rules defining how data should be communicated between devices on a network.
A Standard is an agreed upon specification that describes how a protocol or technology should work.
* Etherent (802.3)
* Wi-Fi (802.11)

TCPIP Layers
1. Application Layer
Provides protocols for communication between application processes, create and interpret the data.
This is where network communications meet applications.
It defines how application processes format, send and interpret data.
The protocols in this layer define message formats and rules for speecific tasks eg;
  #Browsing web pages (HTTP/HTTPS)
  #Transferring files (FTP/TFTP)
  #Sending/receiving email (SMTP, POP3,IMAP)

2. Transport Layer
This layer provides end to end communication between application processes using port numbers.
Runs mainly on communicating hosts
Protocols on this layer are TCP & UDP
Web Server: port 80
File Server: port 21

3. Internet Layer
This  layer provides end to end communication between hosts across networks using IP addresses and routers
Routers operate here, using the message destination IP address to forward the message towrads its final detination host.
Protocols: IP (IPv4 and IPv6)
           ICMP

4. Network Layer
This layer provides hop to hop delivery within a local network using MAC addresses and switches.
A hop is a one step along the path between 2 devices.
* From one router to host, to the next router or host in the path.
* Switches don't count, a swithc just extends the lacl network, allowing multiple devices to connect.
Uses MAC addresses to identify interfaces.

5. Physical Layer
This layer sends bits as electrical, optical or radio signals over the physical medium.
*Cables (copper, UTP, fiber optic, Wi-Fi radios and antennas, NICs)>

Data + L4 header = Segment (TCP) and Datagram (UDP). (L4PDU)
Data + L4 header + L3 header = Packet. (L3PDU).
Data + L4 header + L3 header + L2 header = Frame (L2PDU).

Payloads are contents of each PDU (Everything encapsulated by that layer).
